insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 1, 'A block of Ekene sand has pore volume 2500 m3 and water saturation 0.35. What is the oil volume?', '["875 m3, the pore volume multiplied by the water saturation", "1625 m3, the pore volume multiplied by one minus the water saturation", "2500 m3, because the block sits in the oil column", "500 m3, the pore volume multiplied by the movable fraction 0.4 divided by two"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With two phases the saturations close: So = 1 - Sw = 0.65, and the oil volume is 2500 x 0.65 = 1625 m3. The 875 m3 figure is the water volume, and pore volume itself is not a fluid volume until a saturation multiplies it.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 2, 'In the water-wet Ekene sand, where does the connate water at Swc = 0.35 physically sit?', '["As a continuous layer floating above the oil within each pore", "Dissolved in the oil phase at reservoir temperature", "In pockets that the flood will produce first, since water flows more easily than oil", "As films on grain surfaces and in the finest pores and corners"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Wettability puts the wetting phase on the mineral surfaces and in the smallest openings. That water is disconnected from any flowing pathway, which is exactly why it is immobile and why the water flow at Sw = 0.35 is zero.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 3, 'What is the movable saturation fraction of the Ekene sand, given Swc = 0.35 and Sor = 0.25?', '["0.4", "0.65, the initial oil saturation", "0.75, the top of the mobile window", "0.6, one minus the movable window width"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mobile window runs from Swc to 1 - Sor, so its width is 1 - 0.35 - 0.25 = 0.4. The other numbers are real quantities of this sand, but none of them is the movable fraction.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 4, 'The Ekene pore volume is 22410845.5314109 bbl. Which statement about the movable oil is correct?', '["It is 14567049.6 bbl, the pore volume times the initial oil saturation of 0.65", "It cannot be computed without the injection rate", "It is 8964338.21256436 bbl, the pore volume times 0.4", "It is 5602711.38 bbl, the pore volume times the residual oil saturation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Movable oil is pore volume times the movable fraction 1 - Swc - Sor = 0.4. The endpoints alone set this ceiling; rate and pattern decide how fast and how completely the flood approaches it, never the ceiling itself.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 5, 'Why can a waterflood not recover the residual oil at Sor = 0.25?', '["That oil is too viscous to move at reservoir temperature", "That oil is disconnected into blobs held by interfacial forces far stronger than field pressure gradients", "That oil sits below the oil water contact in the water leg, where injection water never displaces anything", "That oil has already been produced during primary depletion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Residual oil is a trapping phenomenon: water surrounds oil in pore centres and snaps it into disconnected blobs. At pore scale the capillary forces holding each blob dwarf any viscous gradient a flood can apply, so the oil is stranded by geometry, not by viscosity or location.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 6, 'For a water-wet rock, drainage is defined as:', '["the water saturation decreasing", "any process where oil is produced from the rock", "the water saturation increasing", "fluid movement caused by gravity alone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The names attach to the wetting phase''s direction. Drainage is the wetting phase decreasing and imbibition is the wetting phase increasing, regardless of which fluid is injected or produced.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 7, 'Classify the two legs of the Ekene saturation history: oil charging the trap, then the waterflood.', '["Both legs are drainage, since oil moves in both", "Imbibition then drainage", "Both legs are imbibition, since water is present in both", "Drainage then imbibition"]'::jsonb, 3, 'During charge the wetting water is forced down from Sw = 1 toward 0.35, which is drainage. During the flood water returns, rising toward 0.75, which is imbibition. The two legs follow different curve families, which is why history matters.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 8, 'The Ekene design table gives kroMax = 0.9 and krwMax = 0.3. What do these two numbers describe?', '["The fraction of pore volume each fluid occupies at its own endpoint", "How freely each fluid flows when the other is at its immobile endpoint saturation", "The Corey exponents that set the curvature of each relative permeability curve", "The viscosity ratio between oil and water at flood conditions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'kroMax is oil''s relative permeability at connate water and krwMax is water''s at residual oil: each fluid''s flow capacity when it has the mobile window to itself. The exponents nw and no are separate parameters, and viscosities live in a different row of the table.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 9, 'The pore volume 22410845.5314109 bbl comes from converting 3563045.809312045 m3. What conversion factor does the fixture use?', '["6.2898 bbl per m3", "5.615 bbl per m3", "1.2 rb per stb", "0.3048 bbl per m3"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fixture family always converts at 6.2898 barrels per cubic metre. The 5.615 figure is cubic feet per barrel, 1.2 is the Ekene formation volume factor, and 0.3048 is metres per foot: all real constants, none of them this conversion.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 10, 'At the initial Ekene condition Sw = 0.35, what fraction of the flowing stream is water?', '["0.35, equal to the water saturation", "0.4, equal to the movable fraction", "Zero, because the connate water is immobile", "It depends on the injection rate chosen for the flood"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Saturation is where fluids sit, not what they do. At connate saturation the water is disconnected film and corner water, so its flow is exactly zero even though it fills 35 percent of the pore space. The number that gives water''s share of flow is the fractional flow, built in module 3.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 11, 'A flood raises Sw in a 2500 m3 pore-volume cell from 0.35 to 0.55. In this incompressible displacement, how much oil left the cell?', '["1375 m3", "500 m3, equal to the water volume that entered", "875 m3", "No oil leaves until the water saturation reaches 0.75"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The saturation change is 0.2, so 2500 x 0.2 = 500 m3 of oil left and 500 m3 of water arrived: the swap is barrel for barrel. 1375 m3 is the final water volume and 875 m3 the initial one, not the exchange.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 12, 'Which statement about Sor is consistent with this course''s physics?', '["Sor falls if the flood is run at a higher injection pressure", "Sor applies only to the drainage direction of the curves", "Sor is the oil saturation at which the flood becomes uneconomic", "Sor is a rock and fluid constant that operating strategy does not change"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Within this course and its engine, residual oil saturation is a property of the rock and fluid pair, measured on core. Injecting harder does not move it, it belongs to the imbibition direction, and it is a physical trapping limit rather than an economic cutoff.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 13, 'The Ekene relative permeability set is described as an imbibition set. What does that choice mean for the course?', '["The curves describe water displacing oil, the only direction the flood ever moves the saturation", "The curves describe the original oil charge of the trap", "The engine models both directions and switches curve sets automatically at each saturation reversal during the flood", "The curves apply only below the bubble point pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A waterflood raises the wetting-phase saturation, which is imbibition, so the one curve set the engine carries is the waterflood direction. Hysteresis is deliberately not modelled: a design lock that trades reversal processes for traceability.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 14, 'Why does the fixture quote values like 8964338.21256436 bbl at full precision instead of rounding?', '["Field measurements of movable oil are accurate to the last barrel", "The grading engine works at machine precision, and re-rounded values drift in the last figures", "Regulatory reporting conventions require operators to file recoverable volumes at fifteen significant figures", "Rounded values would understate the reserves of the field"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture is exact by construction, so every graded number has one true value at machine precision. Quoting and reusing the printed full-precision value keeps your arithmetic on the engine''s answer; re-rounding and re-multiplying introduces drift that has nothing to do with measurement accuracy.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-two-phases-one-rock', 15, 'A colleague says the Ekene flood can recover 0.65 of the pore volume as oil because So = 0.65 at the start. What is the correct ceiling and why?', '["0.65, because all oil above the connate water saturation is connected and therefore movable by a well designed flood", "0.75, the top of the mobile saturation window", "0.35, because water occupies the rest of the pore space", "0.4 of the pore volume, because Sor = 0.25 of it will be trapped and left behind"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Of the 0.65 oil saturation, 0.25 is destined to become disconnected residual oil in even perfectly swept rock. The displacement prize is the movable window 1 - Swc - Sor = 0.4 of pore volume; 0.75 is the ceiling on water saturation, not on recovered oil.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 1, 'A core plug has absolute permeability 250 md. With both fluids present, the laboratory measures an effective water permeability of 13.2 md. What is the relative permeability to water?', '["13.2, because relative permeability keeps the units of the measurement", "0.0528, the effective permeability divided by the absolute permeability of the plug", "236.8 md, the flow capacity the water phase has surrendered to interference from the oil", "0.947, the fraction of the pore network the water phase cannot reach at this saturation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Relative permeability is the dimensionless ratio of effective to absolute permeability: 13.2/250 = 0.0528. It strips the rock''s plumbing out of the measurement so the sharing physics can travel between rocks.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 2, 'At an intermediate saturation in the Ekene sand, krw + kro sums to roughly 0.28. What does that sum tell you?', '["The rock is damaged, since a healthy water wet sand sums close to 1 at every saturation", "The measurement is unconverged and the laboratory should repeat the displacement", "Water is carrying 28 percent of the flowing stream, since flow shares are read directly off the kr curves", "Nothing physical by itself: the sum of the two kr curves carries no defined meaning at all"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two curves answer two separate questions and their sum has no physical meaning. Each phase interferes with the other, so the sum falls well short of 1 across the interior of the mobile window. Flow shares come from fractional flow, which weighs the curves by viscosity, not from adding kr values.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 3, 'For the Ekene endpoints Swc 0.35 and Sor 0.25, what is the denominator of the normalized saturation Swn?', '["0.4, the width of the mobile saturation window", "0.6, one minus the connate water plus residual oil difference", "0.65, the span from connate water up to full water saturation", "0.75, the saturation where the water curve reaches its endpoint value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Swn = (Sw - Swc)/(1 - Swc - Sor), and 1 - 0.35 - 0.25 = 0.4. That denominator is also the movable fraction of pore volume, which is why the normalization and the flood target are the same number.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 4, 'In the Corey model, which parts of the two curves do the exponents nw and no control?', '["The endpoint saturations, moving the walls of the mobile window inward or outward", "The endpoint permeability values krwMax and kroMax, which the exponents scale across the window", "The interior shape only, with both endpoints of each curve pinned", "The viscosity contrast between the two flowing phases"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Since 0 to any power is 0 and 1 to any power is 1, changing an exponent bends the interior of the curve while both ends stay fixed. Endpoint values scale the curves; exponents shape them. Viscosity is not a rel perm parameter at all.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 5, 'Raise the Ekene water exponent from 2.5 to 4 while holding everything else fixed. What happens to the water curve?', '["The whole curve, endpoints included, scales down by the ratio of the two exponents applied to the endpoint", "The curve rises in the interior because a larger exponent amplifies every value it touches", "The curve is unchanged, because only the oil curve responds to the water exponent", "Interior values drop everywhere between the endpoints, while the endpoints themselves stay put"]'::jsonb, 3, 'For interior saturations Swn is below 1, and a larger power makes a number below 1 smaller, so the curve sags everywhere between its pinned endpoints. The oil curve carries its own exponent and does not respond to nw.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 6, 'Using the Ekene Corey set, what is krw at Sw = 0.55, the midpoint of the mobile window?', '["0.2249999999999999, from the oil branch of the model at the same saturation", "0.15, which is the endpoint 0.3 scaled by the half filled mobile window", "0.05303300858899109, which is 0.3 times 0.5 to the power 2.5, the committed water exponent at work", "0.075, which is 0.3 times 0.5 squared with the exponent misread as 2"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Swn is 0.5 at the midpoint, and krw = 0.3 x 0.5^2.5 = 0.05303300858899109. The value 0.2249999999999999 is kro at the same saturation, and 0.15 comes from treating the exponent as 1. The exponent 2.5 holds water to under eighteen percent of its endpoint at mid window.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 7, 'The engine reports Swn = 0.5000000000000001 at Sw = 0.55 where hand arithmetic gives exactly 0.5. What is the correct reading of that trailing digit?', '["The engine carries a calibration offset that should be subtracted before use", "Binary floating point arithmetic on decimals with no exact binary form; both answers agree at every figure that matters", "The fixture was generated with a slightly different connate water saturation than the one documented", "It signals that Sw = 0.55 sits outside the mobile window by a rounding margin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'None of 0.55, 0.35, or 0.4 has an exact binary representation, so the engine''s last bit differs from the pencil answer. It is an artifact of representation, not physics, and the honest response is to recognize it rather than silently round it away.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 8, 'From the Ekene curve table, what is kro at Sw = 0.60?', '["0.09264485332524548, read from the water column at the same saturation", "0.1265625, which is 0.9 times 0.375 squared", "0.3515625, the oil value one table row higher at Sw = 0.50 carried forward", "0.225, the midpoint oil value carried one row too far down the table"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At Sw 0.60, Swn = 0.625 and kro = 0.9 x (1 - 0.625)^2 = 0.9 x 0.140625 = 0.1265625. The first option is krw at that saturation, and 0.3515625 is kro one table row earlier at Sw 0.50.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 9, 'Doubling krwMax from 0.3 to 0.6 while keeping the exponents fixed does what to the water curve?', '["The whole curve doubles, interior and endpoint alike", "Raises only the endpoint value itself, while interior points keep the values the exponent gave them", "Steepens the curve near connate water but leaves the top half of the window unchanged", "Shifts the curve toward lower saturations without changing any of its heights"]'::jsonb, 0, 'krwMax multiplies the power law, so the entire curve doubles, interior and endpoint alike. The shape is untouched because the exponent did not change. This is the scale half of the shape versus scale division of labour.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 10, 'A submitted kr table shows krw = 0.02 in its lowest saturation row. Which message does the engine''s validation return?', '["kro must be non-increasing in Sw.", "Duplicate Sw values.", "krw at the lowest Sw should be 0 (connate water immobile).", "A rel-perm table needs at least 3 rows."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The first row''s saturation is read as connate water, and connate water is by definition the water that does not flow. A positive krw there claims flowing water at a saturation the table itself labels immobile, and the engine rejects the contradiction with exactly that message.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 11, 'A neighbouring sand matches Ekene everywhere except Sor, which is 0.15 instead of 0.25. What changes?', '["The curves keep their original window but both permeability endpoints drop by 0.1 to conserve the frame", "The mobile window now ends at Sw = 0.85 and the movable fraction grows from 0.4 up to 0.5", "Only the water exponent must be refitted, because residual oil is a shape parameter", "The connate water saturation must fall by the same 0.1 so the window keeps its width"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Residual oil sets the upper wall of the window at 1 - Sor. Lowering Sor to 0.15 moves that wall to 0.85 and widens the movable fraction to 1 - 0.35 - 0.15 = 0.5 of pore volume, a quarter more displacement target with no change in curve shape.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 12, 'How does the engine determine Swc and Sor from a tabular curve set that declares neither?', '["It fits a Corey model to the table first and reads both endpoints from the fitted parameter set", "It requires the user to supply the endpoint saturations alongside the table before it will compute", "Swc is read from the first row''s saturation and Sor as one minus the last tabulated saturation", "It takes the saturation where the two curves cross as the window midpoint and works outward"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A table implies its frame: the lowest tabulated saturation is taken as connate water and one minus the highest as residual oil. That makes the first and last rows declarations rather than ordinary data points, which is why where a table stops matters as much as what it contains.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 13, 'What is the minimum number of rows the engine accepts in a relative permeability table?', '["3", "2, one row for each endpoint of the mobile window", "5, enough to resolve the curvature between the two endpoints", "There is no minimum; a single measured row is accepted with a warning attached"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The validator requires at least 3 rows. Two rows could only draw straight lines between endpoints, and the checker''s floor is a bookkeeping rule rather than a statement that three rows describe a rock well: density between the endpoints is what makes interpolation faithful.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 14, 'The Ekene model sampled at only Sw = 0.35, 0.55, and 0.75 gives a valid three row table. Interpolating it linearly at Sw = 0.45 returns krw = 0.026516504294495546. How does this compare with the true model value?', '["It matches to machine precision, since linear interpolation is exact between any pair of tabulated rows", "It understates the truth, because straight line segments must run below a curve rising toward its endpoint", "It overstates the truth by nearly a factor of three; the model''s own value there is 0.009375000000000003", "The two cannot be compared, since tables and models carry different endpoint conventions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The water curve bows downward between connate water and the midpoint, so the straight segment from 0 to 0.05303300858899109 rides above the curve. Sparse tables cut corners on bowed curves; density restores fidelity.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-relative-permeability', 15, 'When does a laboratory table genuinely serve better than a fitted Corey model?', '["When the curves carry a plateau, a kink, or a shoulder that no power law follows", "Whenever the table has more rows than the model has free parameters to fit them", "Never: the model is guaranteed monotonic and therefore always safer than measured data", "When the absolute permeability of the measured plug falls below about 100 md"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Some rocks produce features no power law can follow, and forcing the model onto them erases exactly what the laboratory measured. The engine accepts tables everywhere a curve set is needed so the reference can stay faithful; the model earns its place through convenience, not authority.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 1, 'The Ekene water saturation at some point in the sand is exactly at connate, Sw = 0.35. What does the engine report for the fractional flow of water there?', '["About 0.06, the first value on the mobile ladder", "A small positive value set by the water viscosity", "Exactly zero: the krw guard returns 0 before the formula runs", "It is undefined, because the formula places krw in a denominator and a division by zero cannot be evaluated"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At connate water the water phase is immobile, krw = 0. The engine guards this case explicitly and returns fw = 0 rather than evaluating the formula, which matches the physics: none of the flowing stream can be water if water cannot flow. The division never happens, so the value is not undefined.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 2, 'In the horizontal case with capillary pressure neglected, which set of quantities fully determines fw at a given saturation?', '["The two relative permeabilities at that saturation and the viscosity ratio", "The two relative permeabilities, the absolute permeability, and the total rate", "The pressure gradient across the element, the viscosity ratio, and the rock porosity at that point", "The endpoint saturations Swc and Sor together with the injection rate the operator chooses to apply"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing the water Darcy law by the total flow cancels the shared pressure gradient and the absolute permeability. What survives is the competition between krw/muW and kro/muO, so only the local kr pair and the viscosity ratio matter. Rate and absolute permeability never appear in the horizontal fw.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 3, 'An operator doubles the total injection rate on a horizontal element of the Ekene flood. What happens to fw evaluated at Sw = 0.6?', '["It doubles, since twice the water is entering the element", "It rises, but by less than a factor of two because oil also speeds up", "It falls, because the oil phase responds to the higher gradient first", "Nothing at all: the shared pressure gradient cancelled, so rate never entered the horizontal fw"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The horizontal fractional flow equation contains no rate term. Both phases share the same pressure gradient, and that gradient cancels when the water rate is divided by the total. fw(0.6) stays at 0.7249143467053674 regardless of how hard the element is pushed. Rate re-enters only through the dip term, which is zero here.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 4, 'Using the Ekene endpoints, krwMax 0.3, kroMax 0.9, muW 0.5 cp, muO 1.8 cp, the endpoint mobility ratio M is', '["0.8333, the same ratio taken upside down", "1.2", "0.6, the water phase''s endpoint mobility", "2.4, the value after doubling the water endpoint"]'::jsonb, 1, 'M = (krwMax/muW)/(kroMax/muO) = (0.3/0.5)/(0.9/1.8) = 0.6/0.5 = 1.2. The value 0.8333 is the ratio upside down, 0.6 is water''s mobility alone before dividing by oil''s 0.5, and 2.4 is what M becomes only if the water endpoint is doubled to 0.6.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 5, 'In the endpoint mobility ratio, at which saturations are the two relative permeabilities taken?', '["Both at the midpoint of the mobile saturation range, halfway between connate water and residual oil", "krw at Sw = 1 - Sor and kro at Sw = Swc: each fluid is scored at the saturation where it is strongest", "Both at the front saturation, where the displacement actually operates", "krw at Swc and kro at 1 - Sor, the saturations where each curve begins its rise from zero"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The convention scores each fluid where it is most mobile: water behind a complete flood at 1 - Sor, where krw reaches its endpoint, and oil ahead of the flood at connate water, where kro peaks. Taking both at one shared saturation gives a local ratio, a different object, and at Swc the water curve is zero, not at its best.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 6, 'For the textbook sample case (Swc 0.2, Sor 0.2, krwMax 0.4, kroMax 1.0, both exponents 2, muW 0.5, muO 5), the fractional flow at Sw = 0.5 works out by hand to', '["0.5, matching the normalized saturation", "0.459029062228061", "0.25", "0.8, from krw 0.1 and kro 0.25"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The normalized saturation is 0.5, so krw = 0.4 x 0.25 = 0.1 and kro = 1.0 x 0.25 = 0.25. Then fw = 1/(1 + (0.25 x 0.5)/(0.1 x 5)) = 1/1.25 = 0.8. The value 0.459029062228061 belongs to the Ekene sand at Sw = 0.55, and 0.25 is this rock''s kro, not its fractional flow.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 7, 'Two rocks share the same endpoint saturations and endpoint kr values but have different Corey exponents. What does the endpoint mobility ratio say about them?', '["It is identical for both, since M is built from endpoints only", "It differs in proportion to the ratio of their water exponents", "It is larger for the rock whose curves bend more steeply between the endpoints, because curvature adds mobility", "It cannot be computed until the exponents are known"]'::jsonb, 0, 'M uses krwMax, kroMax and the two viscosities and nothing else. The exponents shape the middle of the curves and therefore the fractional flow between the pins, so the two rocks flood differently, but their M is one and the same number. That is exactly why M is a headline rather than the whole story.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 8, 'Reading the Ekene fw ladder in 0.05 steps, which interval carries the largest gain in fw?', '["0.45 to 0.50, where the curve first leaves its floor", "0.50 to 0.55", "0.55 to 0.60, the heart of the steep middle", "0.65 to 0.70, the final approach to the ceiling"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gains are about 0.15 from 0.45 to 0.50, about 0.25 from 0.50 to 0.55, about 0.27 from 0.55 to 0.60, and only about 0.08 from 0.65 to 0.70. The steep middle of the S sits in the 0.55 to 0.60 step, where fw climbs from 0.459029062228061 to 0.7249143467053674.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 9, 'Why does the Ekene fractional flow curve sit exactly at 1 for every saturation at or above 0.75?', '["Because the water viscosity advantage becomes infinite there", "Because kro is zero at and beyond residual oil, so the guard returns exactly 1", "Because the Corey exponents force the two curves to cross exactly at that saturation for this rock", "It does not: the curve only approaches 1 asymptotically as water floods the last accessible pores, and it never quite reaches it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At Sw = 1 - Sor = 0.75 the remaining oil is at residual saturation and immobile, kro = 0. The engine''s guard returns fw = 1 exactly, and the physics agrees: if oil cannot move, the entire flowing stream is water. The pin is exact, not asymptotic, which is why no viscosity choice in the panel can move it.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 10, 'The fractional flow curve is best described as', '["a fixed property of the rock and fluids that the flood moves along", "a record of the water cut measured at the producing well, plotted against cumulative time as the flood matures", "a forecast that must be recomputed each year as the flood matures and the saturations redistribute", "a laboratory artifact that only applies at core scale"]'::jsonb, 0, 'fw(Sw) is fixed by the kr curves and the viscosities before any water is injected; it carries no time axis. A producing well traces a path along the curve as its outlet saturation evolves, which is the module 4 story. Nothing about the curve itself changes as the flood matures, and the same function governs core and field element alike under the 1-D assumptions.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 11, 'From the Ekene ladder, the fractional flow at Sw = 0.60 is', '["0.9034103334774292", "0.459029062228061", "0.20920166128387394", "0.7249143467053674, straight from the engine ladder"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine gives fw(0.60) = 0.7249143467053674. The other three values are real points on the same curve in the wrong roles: 0.9034103334774292 belongs to Sw 0.65, 0.459029062228061 to Sw 0.55, and 0.20920166128387394 to Sw 0.50.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 12, 'Raising the Ekene oil viscosity from 1.8 cp to 10 cp with the rock curves unchanged does what to the fw curve?', '["Shifts it down and to the right, since thicker oil holds the water back and delays its arrival at every saturation", "Steepens it symmetrically about its midpoint without shifting it, since only the exponents can translate the curve", "Shifts it up and left across the mobile range; the two pins stay fixed", "Moves the right hand pin from 0.75 toward 0.70"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Oil viscosity enters only the denominator term kro muW / (krw muO), so a larger muO shrinks that term at every saturation and raises fw everywhere in the mobile range: up and to the left. The pins cannot move, because they are set by krw = 0 at 0.35 and kro = 0 at 0.75, and saturation endpoints know nothing of viscosity.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 13, 'The Ekene mobility ratio is 1.2 at muO = 1.8 cp. With everything else unchanged, at muO = 10 cp the mobility ratio is', '["6.666666666666667, scaling 1.2 by 10/1.8", "3.3333333333333335", "12", "still 1.2, because M depends only on the rock endpoints"]'::jsonb, 0, 'M is directly proportional to muO when nothing else changes: M = 1.2 x (10/1.8) = 6.666666666666667. The value 3.3333333333333335 is the muO = 5 cp case, and 12 comes from multiplying by 10 instead of by the viscosity ratio. M does depend on viscosities, not endpoints alone.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 14, 'Across the three viscosity rows of the sensitivity table (muO 1.8, 5, and 10 cp), the maximum displacement efficiency EDmax', '["falls in step with the breakthrough efficiency, since both ultimately measure how completely water displaces the oil", "It is 0.6153846153846154 in every row: the endpoint saturations alone set it", "rises slightly, since more water passes through the system", "cannot be compared across the rows, because efficiencies measured at different viscosities are on different bases"]'::jsonb, 1, 'EDmax is a function of Swc and Sor alone, and neither changed between rows. Viscosity governs how quickly and at what water cost the flood approaches the ceiling, never the ceiling itself. That separation, speed versus ceiling, is the honest reading of the whole table.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-fractional-flow', 15, 'In the muO = 10 cp row the front saturation is 0.5327999999999999, against 0.6372 for the base oil. The correct reading is that heavier oil', '["raises the saturation the front must reach before it can begin to move through the element", "leaves the front saturation alone but delays its arrival", "lowers the front saturation, so the arriving front stores less water behind it", "eliminates the sharp front entirely, smearing the displacement into a gradual transition that never produces a distinct breakthrough"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The up-and-left shift of the fw curve moves the tangent geometry of module 4 to a lower saturation, so the front forms and travels at Swf = 0.5327999999999999 instead of 0.6372. A lower front saturation means less water stored behind the front when it arrives, which is why breakthrough comes earlier and the breakthrough efficiency is lower. The front still exists; the S shape guarantees it.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 1, 'How does the engine locate the front saturation on the fractional flow curve?', '["It integrates the saturation profile from the injector forward and places the front where the profile becomes multivalued", "It finds where the fractional flow curve crosses the value 0.5", "It keeps the saturation with the largest secant slope from (Swc, 0)", "It solves for the saturation where krw equals kro and reads the front there"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The search maximizes fw/(Sw - Swc) over a grid of candidates. A line from the anchor that cuts the curve twice is never the steepest, so the maximum secant can only touch the curve, and touching means its slope equals the curve''s derivative there. The multivalued-profile picture is a way of deriving the theory, but it is not what the engine computes.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 2, 'The Welge tangent for the Ekene sand is anchored at which point?', '["The origin (0, 0), since a displacement calculation must start from zero saturation and zero flow", "(0.35, 0), connate water at zero fractional flow", "(0.75, 1), the endpoint at residual oil where fw reaches one", "(0.6372, 0.8682763300877854)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The construction begins where the displacement begins: ahead of the front the rock carries immobile connate water at Swc = 0.35 with no flowing water. Anchoring at the origin gives a confidently wrong front. The point (0.6372, 0.8682763300877854) is the touch point the construction finds, not its anchor.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 3, 'What is the front saturation Swf for the Ekene displacement?', '["0.6372", "0.6807702744481854", "0.75, the top of the mobile saturation window", "0.35"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tangent from (0.35, 0) touches the Ekene curve at Sw = 0.6372. The value 0.6807702744481854 is the average saturation behind the front at breakthrough, a different quantity; 0.75 is the residual oil endpoint and 0.35 is connate water.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 4, 'The day the Ekene front reaches a producer, roughly what water fraction does the producing stream jump to at reservoir conditions?', '["About 0.33, matching the pore volumes injected by that day", "About 0.51, matching the displacement efficiency at breakthrough", "Essentially 1.0, since the front arrives as a bank of nearly pure injection water", "About 0.87"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The front carries fwf = 0.8682763300877854, so breakthrough takes the well from dry oil to roughly seven barrels of water in every eight of gross rate in one step. The front is not pure water: oil still flows on both sides of it. The 0.33 figure is injected pore volumes, not a water fraction.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 5, 'The tangent slope on the Ekene curve is 3.023246274678918. What is the breakthrough time in pore volumes injected?', '["3.023246274678918 PV, the slope read directly", "0.33077027444818546 PV", "0.2872 PV", "0.4134628430602318 PV"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Breakthrough occurs at QiBt = 1 divided by the tangent slope, 1/3.023246274678918 = 0.33077027444818546 pore volumes. Quoting the slope itself confuses a speed for a time; 0.2872 is the saturation jump at the front, and 0.4134628430602318 is the breakthrough of a rock with Sor = 0.15, not this one.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 6, 'Why must the average water saturation behind the front exceed the front saturation itself?', '["The swept zone grades from the front value up toward 1 - Sor at the injector", "Water is denser than oil, so gravity segregation raises the average behind the front above its leading edge value", "It does not: at breakthrough the two are equal by construction", "The tangent line always lies above the fractional flow curve, which lifts the average"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Behind the shock the profile runs from Swf = 0.6372 at the face up toward 0.75 at the injector. An average over values that start at the front saturation and rise from there has to exceed the front saturation. Density plays no role in the one dimensional theory, and the two values are never equal on a curved fw.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 7, 'Using the Welge identity, the average saturation behind the Ekene front at breakthrough equals Swc plus the pore volumes injected. What is its value?', '["0.6372, since the swept zone equilibrates to the front value", "0.75", "0.6807702744481854", "0.5088773453049006"]'::jsonb, 2, 'SwAvgBt = 0.35 + 0.33077027444818546 = 0.6807702744481854. Geometrically it is where the extended tangent line crosses fw = 1. The swept zone never equilibrates to the front value, 0.75 is the endpoint at residual oil, and 0.5088773453049006 is the displacement efficiency, which is not a saturation at all.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 8, 'What is the displacement efficiency of the Ekene flood at breakthrough?', '["0.6153846153846154", "0.33077027444818546", "0.44184615384615383, computed from the front saturation as (0.6372 - 0.35)/0.65", "0.5088773453049006"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Recovery is a statement about the whole swept volume, so it uses the average: EDbt = (0.6807702744481854 - 0.35)/0.65 = 0.5088773453049006. The front-based figure 0.44184615384615383 is the module''s classic error and understates recovery by about 6.7 percentage points. The value 0.6153846153846154 is the ultimate ceiling, not the breakthrough value.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 9, 'How large is the saturation jump across the Ekene flood front?', '["0.4, the full width of the mobile saturation window", "0.2872", "0.1128, the spread of the smooth ramp between the front and the injector endpoint", "0.6372"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shock leaps from 0.35 ahead of the face to 0.6372 behind it, a jump of 0.2872. The 0.1128 interval is the ramp behind the front, which is smooth rather than part of the jump, and 0.6372 is the front saturation itself rather than a difference.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 10, 'What does the rock ahead of the advancing front contain?', '["Immobile connate water at 0.35, with oil carrying all the flow", "A mixture of injected and connate water at steadily growing saturation as the front approaches", "Only oil, with no water of any kind present in the pores", "Residual oil at 0.25 with water flowing past it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ahead of the shock the reservoir has not yet seen injection water: it sits at Swc = 0.35, which is immobile, while oil flows. Saturation there does not grow until the front arrives, and no rock is ever free of water. Residual oil with flowing water describes the swept region far behind the front.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 11, 'The fractional flow curve is perfectly smooth, yet the displacement develops a discontinuity. What makes the shock form?', '["Capillary forces at the leading edge steepen the profile until it becomes a discontinuity", "The injection rate exceeds what the rock can transmit, so water piles up at a face", "Numerical error in the engine accumulates at the front as the grid refines", "Fast mid-range saturations would overtake slow low ones, and the profile collapses into a jump"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each saturation travels at a speed proportional to dfw/dSw at that saturation. The steep middle of the S curve outruns the flat toe, the profile tries to become multivalued, and the physical resolution is a sharp front. The theory used here neglects capillary pressure entirely, and the shock is exact mathematics, not numerical error or a rate effect.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 12, 'The Ekene front saturation is reported as 0.6372. Which statement about this number''s precision is correct?', '["All four decimals are physical properties of the rock and fluid system", "The number is exact to all digits because the teaching data contains no noise", "The search steps by 0.0004, so 0.6372 is grid point 718 and its last digit is resolution", "The trailing digits carry measurement uncertainty forward from the core laboratory"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine scans 1000 steps across the mobile window from 0.35 to 0.75, a spacing of (0.75 - 0.35)/1000 = 0.0004, and reports the best grid point: 0.35 + 718 x 0.0004 = 0.6372. Refining the grid could shift the last digit without any change in the rock. The leading digits are physics; the tail is the contour interval of the search.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 13, 'Why is breakthrough on a favorable flood like Ekene an abrupt event at the producer rather than a slow rise in water cut?', '["Because injection and production begin at the same time, water arrives in one coordinated wave", "The shock carries fractional flow near 0.87, so the well steps to mostly water at once", "Water cut can only change when the injection rate at the pattern is changed", "The lift equipment at the producer responds nonlinearly once free water appears in the tubing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'No intermediate saturations travel ahead of the front, so the well sees connate conditions right up to arrival, then the front''s own fractional flow, 0.8682763300877854, immediately after. The sharpness is a property of the displacement, not of equipment, timing or rate schedules.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 14, 'Oil viscosity on the Ekene sand is raised from 1.8 cp to 10 cp with everything else unchanged. What happens to the front?', '["Swf falls to 0.5327999999999999 and breakthrough moves earlier, to 0.24006333128830806 PV", "The front saturation rises toward 0.75 and breakthrough is delayed past half a pore volume", "The front saturation is unchanged because it is fixed by the relative permeability curves alone", "Breakthrough in pore volumes is unchanged because pore volume does not depend on viscosity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Heavier oil steepens the early part of the fw curve, and the tangent then touches lower: Swf drops to 0.5327999999999999 with QiBt = 0.24006333128830806 PV. The front depends on the whole fw curve, which contains both viscosities, so it cannot be indifferent to muO even though the pore volume itself is.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-welge-tangent', 15, 'At Sw = 0.65 the secant slope from (0.35, 0) is 3.0113677782580965, within half a percent of the maximum 3.023246274678918 at 0.6372. What does this closeness tell you?', '["The front is a band of saturations between 0.6372 and 0.65 travelling together as a smeared shock", "The engine''s search failed to converge and split the difference between two candidates", "The peak is flat, so a coarse grid lands near the true tangency; the front stays one point", "The two saturations alternate at the front as the flood advances through the pattern"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A flat peak means neighboring candidates score nearly the same, which makes the grid search robust: bracketing error is small. It does not blur the answer, because tangency is a single exact condition on the curve. The front is one saturation; 0.65 simply loses the comparison by a small margin.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 1, 'In the Welge construction, the cumulative injection Qi in pore volumes at the moment the outlet reaches saturation Sw2 is computed as which of the following?', '["The outlet fractional flow fw(Sw2) divided by the front saturation Swf", "The area under the fractional flow curve between the connate saturation and Sw2", "The reciprocal of the slope of the fractional flow curve at Sw2", "The reciprocal of the outlet fractional flow fw(Sw2)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Welge relation is Qi = 1/fw''(Sw2): the pore volumes injected are the reciprocal of the fractional flow derivative at the outlet saturation. Neither the fractional flow value itself nor any area under the curve appears in the relation, which is why the recovery profile is built by evaluating the derivative at each outlet saturation.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 2, 'Why is the average water saturation behind the front always higher than the saturation at the outlet end of the system?', '["Rock near the injector has flooded longest, so upstream saturations exceed the outlet value", "The numerical grid the engine uses systematically overestimates the average by one cell width at every step", "The outlet has already reached residual oil by the time the average is computed", "The average includes the bank of injected water that travels ahead of the front"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Saturation decreases from inlet to outlet in the displaced zone: rock near the injector has taken water for the longest time. The volumetric average over the swept system therefore exceeds the value at the producing end, and the Welge term (1 - fw)/fw'' quantifies exactly that excess. Nothing travels ahead of the front in the shock model, and the average is an exact relation, not a grid artifact.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 3, 'In the ideal one dimensional displacement of the Ekene sand, what does the water fraction at the producer do at the moment of breakthrough?', '["It rises gradually from zero as the smeared transition zone arrives at the well", "It jumps from zero directly to the front value of 0.8682763300877854", "It jumps to 0.6807702744481854, the average saturation behind the front", "It jumps to one and then declines as fresh oil banks up behind the water"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shock carries the whole saturation jump, so the producing end goes from clean oil to the front''s fractional flow of 0.8682763300877854 in one step. A gradual climb is what layered real wells show, not the single layer ideal case. The value 0.6807702744481854 is a saturation, not a flow fraction, and the water fraction reaches one only as the outlet approaches residual oil.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 4, 'This module quotes displacement efficiencies of about 0.534 at half a pore volume and about 0.565 at one pore volume injected. What is the standing of these two numbers?', '["They are exact closed form evaluations of the Welge relations, reliable to machine precision at those volumes", "They are laboratory measurements taken on the Ekene core plugs", "They are the breakthrough values restated at a coarser rounding", "They are interpolations between engine profile rows; graded work should cite engine rows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns a recovery profile at forty one discrete outlet saturations. Reading it at round injection volumes requires interpolating between rows, so those landmark values are deliberately quoted to three figures only. Anything that must be exact, such as a capstone entry, should come from an engine row or an engine identity, never from an interpolated reading.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 5, 'What is the ultimate displacement efficiency of the base Ekene sand, with connate water 0.35 and residual oil 0.25?', '["0.5088773453049006", "0.6153846153846154", "0.4", "0.6807702744481854"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ceiling is the closed form (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc) = 0.4/0.65 = 0.6153846153846154. The value 0.5088773453049006 is the efficiency already reached at breakthrough, 0.4 is the movable saturation window that forms the numerator, and 0.6807702744481854 is the average water saturation behind the front at breakthrough.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 6, 'If a wettability treatment cut the Ekene residual oil saturation from 0.25 to 0.15 with everything else unchanged, the ultimate displacement efficiency would become:', '["0.7692307692307692", "0.6360966816311258", "0.6153846153846154", "0.5882352941176471"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With the residual cut, the closed form gives (1 - 0.15 - 0.35)/(1 - 0.35) = 0.5/0.65 = 0.7692307692307692. The value 0.6360966816311258 is that case''s efficiency at breakthrough, not its ceiling; 0.6153846153846154 is the unmodified base ceiling; and 0.5882352941176471 is 0.5/0.85, the slip of dividing by one minus the residual oil instead of one minus the connate water.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 7, 'The last row of the Ekene recovery profile shows 3837.600651170649 pore volumes injected to reach an outlet saturation of 0.75. What does this enormous number reflect?', '["The pore volume of the system grows as water replaces the more compressible oil", "The engine''s central difference derivative breaks down at the endpoint and returns numerical noise with no physical meaning", "The fractional flow curve flattens near residual oil, so its slope tends to zero and Qi diverges", "Water viscosity increases with saturation, slowing the flood in its late stages"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Qi = 1/fw'' and the fractional flow curve approaches its ceiling of one with vanishing slope as the outlet nears residual oil, so the reciprocal grows without bound. The derivative is small but meaningful, not noise: it is faithfully reporting that each further increment of displacement costs more injection than the one before. Pore volume and fluid viscosities are constants of the problem.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 8, 'Which of the following changes would raise the ultimate displacement efficiency of the Ekene flood?', '["Cutting the oil viscosity from 1.8 cp to 0.5 cp", "Raising the water endpoint krwMax from 0.3 to 0.6", "Choosing a lower injection rate so the flood has more time to work", "Cutting the residual oil saturation from 0.25 to 0.15"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ceiling (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc) contains only the two endpoint saturations, so only endpoint changes move it. Viscosity and the kr endpoints reshape the road to the ceiling, moving breakthrough and the cost of the tail, and injection rate only rescales the calendar. Cutting Sor to 0.15 lifts the ceiling to 0.7692307692307692.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 9, 'What fraction of the Ekene pore volume is movable oil, the window the displacement can actually recover?', '["0.4", "0.65", "0.25", "0.35"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The movable window is 1 - Swc - Sor = 1 - 0.35 - 0.25 = 0.4 of the pore volume, which on 22410845.5314109 barrels of pore space is 8964338.21256436 barrels. The 0.65 is the oil share of the pore space at start (1 - Swc), while 0.25 and 0.35 are the residual and connate saturations themselves.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 10, 'At a steady injection rate of 8000 barrels of water per day, how many days does the Ekene flood take to reach breakthrough?', '["2801.3556914263622", "926.6051908800841", "1482.5683054081346", "741.2841527040673"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Breakthrough time is QiBt times the pore volume divided by the rate: 0.33077027444818546 x 22410845.5314109 / 8000 = 926.6051908800841 days. The value 2801.3556914263622 is the time for one full pore volume at the same rate, while 1482.5683054081346 and 741.2841527040673 are the breakthrough times at 5000 and 10000 barrels per day respectively.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 11, 'How long does injecting one full pore volume into the Ekene sand take at 5000 barrels of water per day?', '["2801.3556914263622 days", "2241.08455314109 days", "4482.16910628218 days", "1482.5683054081346 days"]'::jsonb, 2, 'One pore volume takes PV divided by rate: 22410845.5314109 / 5000 = 4482.16910628218 days, a little over twelve years. The values 2801.3556914263622 and 2241.08455314109 are the one pore volume times at 8000 and 10000 barrels per day, and 1482.5683054081346 is the breakthrough time, not the full pore volume time, at 5000.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 12, 'The injection rate on the flat Ekene displacement is doubled from 5000 to 10000 barrels of water per day. What happens?', '["Both times halve; the saturation history in pore volume time is unchanged", "Breakthrough arrives in half the time but the displacement efficiency at breakthrough drops", "The recovery profile shifts so that breakthrough needs fewer pore volumes of injection", "Nothing changes at all, because the ideal displacement is completely blind to injection rate in calendar time as well"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the horizontal model, rate never enters the fractional flow equation, so everything expressed in pore volumes is fixed; the conversion to days scales as 1/rate, halving 1482.5683054081346 to 741.2841527040673 days. Efficiency at breakthrough and the pore volume requirement cannot respond to rate. The calendar does change, which is exactly the sense in which the flood is not rate blind.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 13, 'The recovery factor of a real waterflooded field is the product of which efficiencies?', '["Displacement efficiency and the mobility ratio", "Breakthrough efficiency and ultimate efficiency", "Areal sweep and vertical sweep only, since displacement is already counted in each", "Displacement efficiency, areal sweep efficiency, and vertical sweep efficiency"]'::jsonb, 3, 'RF = ED x EA x EV: within swept rock the displacement efficiency applies, and the two sweep efficiencies say how much of the field, in map view and in section, is swept at all. Mobility ratio influences all three factors but is not itself an efficiency, and the two sweep terms do not contain the displacement physics.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 14, 'Where in the academy is the areal sweep of injection patterns taught and computed?', '["In this course''s module on the Welge tangent", "The Waterflood Management course, which imports this displacement mathematics", "In the Material Balance course, through the drive index decomposition", "The Mapping course, since areal sweep is a map view quantity computed from grid surfaces"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The division of labour is deliberate: this course owns the one dimensional displacement, and the waterflood course owns patterns, voidage, surveillance, and the sweep efficiencies, composing its results on top of the displacement physics taught here. This course never asks where the wells are, because in one dimension there is nowhere for them to be except the ends.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-recovery-and-time', 15, 'Which statement correctly characterizes the breakthrough injection figure of 0.33077027444818546 for the Ekene sand?', '["It is the fraction of the oil originally in place that has been recovered at the moment of breakthrough", "It is the water cut at the producer on the day of breakthrough", "Pore volumes injected when the front reaches the outlet; a pore volume and a rate turn it into a date", "It is the fraction of the pattern area swept by water at breakthrough"]'::jsonb, 2, 'QiBt is dimensionless injection: pore volumes of water in when the front crosses the system. The oil in place fraction recovered at breakthrough is the displacement efficiency 0.5088773453049006, the water cut at breakthrough is 0.8682763300877854, and swept area is an areal sweep concept that one dimensional theory does not contain. Converting QiBt to calendar time requires pvToDays with the pore volume and the rate.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'Which quantity in the Ekene displacement is a ceiling that moves only when the saturation endpoints move?', '["The displacement efficiency at breakthrough, 0.5088773453049006", "The fractional flow at the front, 0.8682763300877854", "The ultimate displacement efficiency, (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc)", "The endpoint mobility ratio M, which is 1.2 on the Ekene design"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EDmax equals (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc) = 0.6153846153846154, built from endpoints alone. Viscosities and Corey exponents reshape the curves and move the front, breakthrough timing, and EDbt, but the ceiling stays fixed until Swc or Sor themselves change. M changes with viscosities and endpoint permeabilities.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'A learner submits 0.6807702744481854 for the front saturation and fails that field. What did they actually report?', '["The Welge average saturation behind the front at breakthrough", "The outlet saturation on the recovery profile at one full pore volume injected", "The front saturation from the textbook sample case", "The water saturation at which fw first reaches 0.9"]'::jsonb, 0, 'That value is SwAvgBt, which equals Swc plus QiBt. The front itself sits at 0.6372, and the Welge construction is precisely the machinery that keeps the shock saturation and the average behind it distinct. Confusing them is the classic module 4 error.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'Why does the pore volumes injected at breakthrough equal the reciprocal of the tangent slope?', '["Because the tangent slope is the mobility ratio expressed per pore volume", "Because the recovery profile between breakthrough and residual oil is linear in pore volumes", "Because incompressible flow makes injected volume equal produced volume", "Because a saturation travels at a speed set by the slope of fw there"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Buckley-Leverett theory moves each saturation at a speed proportional to fw'' evaluated there. The front reaches the outlet, a distance of one system length, after injecting 1/fw''(Swf) pore volumes. On Ekene that is 1/3.023246274678918, which is 0.33077027444818546 PV.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'The capstone''s clock field asks for days to breakthrough at a steady 8000 bwpd. Which chain produces the graded answer?', '["Divide the movable oil volume of 8964338.21256436 barrels by the injection rate", "Multiply QiBt by the pore volume in barrels, then divide by the rate", "Divide the pore volume by the rate, then multiply the result by EDbt", "Multiply SwAvgBt by the pore volume in barrels, then divide by the rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Time is injected volume over rate, and the injected volume at breakthrough is QiBt pore volumes: 0.33077027444818546 times 22410845.5314109 bbl over 8000 bwpd gives 926.6051908800841 days. Using movable oil, EDbt, or the average saturation puts a recovery quantity where an injection quantity belongs.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'Which neglected physics is the reason the Buckley-Leverett front is a sharp discontinuity rather than a smeared zone?', '["Capillary pressure", "Gravity acting along the dip of the bed", "Compressibility of the injected water phase", "Layering of the sand between the wells"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fractional flow function used only viscosities and relative permeabilities. The capillary term that would diffuse the shock over a finite distance is deliberately dropped, which is what makes the tangent construction and a single breakthrough time possible.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'The Ekene mobility ratio of 1.2 is computed from which ingredients?', '["The kr values evaluated at the front saturation 0.6372 together with the two viscosities", "The average kr over the mobile saturation window and the two viscosities", "The endpoint kr values of the two phases and the two viscosities", "The Corey exponents and the endpoint saturations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'M is the endpoint mobility ratio: krwMax at residual oil over muW, divided by kroMax at connate water over muO, giving (0.3/0.5)/(0.9/1.8) = 1.2. Evaluating the curves anywhere else, or using exponents, computes a different and ungraded quantity.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'A submission passes the front saturation field but fails displacement efficiency at breakthrough with 0.4418461538461538. What went wrong?', '["The learner divided by 1 - Sor instead of 1 - Swc when forming the efficiency denominator", "The front saturation was used in place of the Welge average", "The learner reported the value from the textbook sample case", "The learner subtracted Sor rather than Swc in the numerator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Substituting Swf gives (0.6372 - 0.35)/0.65 = 0.4418461538461538, exactly the failing value. The graded quantity uses the average behind the front, (0.6807702744481854 - 0.35)/0.65 = 0.5088773453049006. The front is where water arrives; the average is what the swept rock holds.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'Which identity lets you check the average saturation behind the front at breakthrough without opening the recovery table?', '["SwAvgBt equals 1 - Sor minus the front saturation", "SwAvgBt equals fwf divided by the tangent slope of the fractional flow curve", "SwAvgBt equals EDmax multiplied by the front saturation", "SwAvgBt equals Swc plus the pore volumes injected at breakthrough"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Before breakthrough no water is produced, so every injected pore volume is stored behind the front, raising the average saturation there by exactly QiBt above connate: 0.35 + 0.33077027444818546 = 0.6807702744481854. It is a conservation statement, not a coincidence.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'What real-field feature does the one-dimensional assumption hide entirely?', '["Sweep, the rock between wells that injected water never contacts", "The viscosity contrast between the displacing and displaced phases", "The residual oil saturation of the contacted rock", "The shape of the relative permeability curves"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A line from injector to producer has no geometry to miss. Viscosities, residuals, and curve shapes are all inside the model; what it cannot represent is areal or vertical sweep, where uncontacted rock keeps its oil at 1 - Swc. That bookkeeping belongs to the Waterflood course.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'In this model, what does it mean that the displacement is immiscible?', '["The two phases flow at exactly equal velocity through every pore of the rock", "The front travels faster than any individual molecule of injected water", "Each fluid keeps its identity, so saturation fully books where each one is", "The water cannot enter pores below a threshold size"]'::jsonb, 2, 'No mixing or component exchange means a barrel of water stays water and a barrel of oil stays oil, which is exactly why tracking Sw suffices to describe the state of the system. Solvent and gas injection break this and need entirely different machinery.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'The last row of the Ekene recovery profile shows 3837.600651170649 pore volumes injected at an outlet saturation of 0.75. Why is the number so enormous?', '["The engine extrapolates the recovery profile beyond the last physical saturation", "The slope of fw approaches zero near residual oil, and pore volumes are its reciprocal", "The pore volume itself shrinks as oil is removed from the system", "Water recycling doubles the count of injected volumes after breakthrough"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each recovery row carries Qi = 1/fw''(Sw2). As the outlet approaches 1 - Sor the fractional flow curve flattens, its derivative tends to zero, and the reciprocal blows up. The physics reading: the last increments of displaceable oil cost absurd volumes of water, so EDmax is approached asymptotically, never reached.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'Ekene''s graded breakthrough assumes homogeneous rock. In a layered version of the same sand, which graded quantity would real behavior move first, and in which direction?', '["The mobility ratio would rise, because layers multiply the viscosity contrast", "The ultimate displacement efficiency would fall, because layering raises the average residual oil saturation", "The fractional flow at the front would fall, because layers dilute the water", "Breakthrough would arrive earlier than the graded value, because the fastest layer breaches first"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Layers breach in order of speed, so first water arrives when the quickest layer breaks through, before the homogeneous average of 926.6051908800841 days says it should. M, EDmax, and fwf are rock and fluid properties unchanged by stacking; the clock is what layering moves, and the water cut then climbs in steps.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'Why does the engine report the Ekene front saturation as exactly 0.6372?', '["The front saturation is defined as 71.8 percent of the distance from connate water to residual oil", "A 1000-step grid search finds the peak secant slope at that grid point", "The value is rounded for display from an internal closed-form solution of the tangency condition", "The Corey exponents force the front onto a multiple of 0.0004"]'::jsonb, 1, 'welgeTangent scans the window from 0.35 to 0.75 in steps of 0.0004 and keeps the saturation maximizing the secant slope from (Swc, 0). The answer is grid-quantized: a setting of the search, not a property of the rock. The 0.0005 grading tolerance is chosen with that in mind.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'At breakthrough the average saturation rise behind the front equals the pore volumes injected. What physical statement makes this true?', '["No water is produced yet, so injection is stored behind the front", "The front saturation equals the arithmetic average of the connate and residual saturations", "The tangent slope equals the mobility ratio evaluated at the front", "Displacement efficiency is constant everywhere ahead of the front"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Until the front reaches the outlet, the producer sees only oil. Conservation then forces every injected barrel to sit behind the front, so the swept region''s average saturation rises by exactly the injected pore volumes. After breakthrough water is produced and the identity no longer holds.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'The movable fraction of the Ekene pore volume is 0.4. Which two numbers produce it?', '["The endpoint permeabilities 0.3 and 0.9", "The water and oil viscosities of 0.5 and 1.8 centipoise", "The bracketing saturations Swc 0.35 and Sor 0.25", "The front saturation 0.6372 together with the ceiling 0.6153846153846154"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Movable fraction is 1 - Swc - Sor = 1 - 0.35 - 0.25 = 0.4, the width of the saturation window where both phases can be displaced. On the 22410845.5314109 bbl pore volume that is 8964338.21256436 bbl of movable oil, fixed before any curve or viscosity enters the problem.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'The Ekene relative permeability set has connate water saturation 0.35 and residual oil saturation 0.25. What fraction of the pore volume is movable oil before the flood starts?', '["0.65", "0.40", "0.60", "0.25"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The movable span is 1 - Swc - Sor = 1 - 0.35 - 0.25 = 0.40. The 0.65 figure is 1 - Swc, which still contains the oil that will be stranded as residual, and 0.25 is that residual itself, the part the flood can never take.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'The Ekene pore volume is 22410845.5314109 bbl. Roughly how much movable oil does the sand hold at the start of the flood?', '["About 9.0 million barrels", "About 14.6 million barrels", "About 22.4 million barrels", "About 5.6 million barrels"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Movable oil is the pore volume times the movable span: 22410845.5314109 x 0.40 = 8964338.21256436 bbl. The 14.6 million figure multiplies by 1 - Swc and forgets residual oil, 22.4 million treats the whole pore volume as movable, and 5.6 million is the residual fraction, the oil that stays.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'In a strongly water-wet rock at connate water saturation, where does the water sit?', '["In the centers of the largest pores, surrounded by oil", "As a continuous layer at the base of the reservoir only", "Coating the grains and filling the smallest pores", "Evenly mixed with the oil as droplets in every pore"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The wetting phase clings to the solid: it films the grains and occupies the tightest pore throats, which is exactly why it is immobile at connate saturation. Pore centers belong to the non-wetting oil, and saturation is a pore-scale property, not a gravity layer.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Saturations in the two-phase Ekene sand always satisfy which relation?', '["Sw + So = 1", "Sw + So = porosity", "Sw - So = Swc", "Sw + So = 1 - Swc"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Saturations are fractions of the pore space, so with only water and oil present they sum to one. Porosity lives at a different bookkeeping level: it relates pore space to bulk rock, not one fluid to another.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'Water displacing oil in a water-wet rock is classed as which saturation process?', '["Drainage, because oil is being removed from the rock", "Drainage, because water enters the sand at the injection wells", "Neither one, because both the oil phase and the water phase are moving at the same time", "Imbibition, because the wetting phase saturation increases"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The process is named for what the WETTING phase does: its saturation rises during a waterflood of a water-wet rock, so this is imbibition. The everyday sense of draining oil out misleads; drainage means the wetting phase is being displaced.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Ekene oil is 3.6 times more viscous than the injected water (1.8 cp against 0.5 cp), yet the endpoint mobility ratio is only 1.2. What holds M down?', '["The connate water: at Swc 0.35 the water phase is completely immobile and contributes no mobility of its own", "The endpoint kr ratio: krwMax 0.3 against kroMax 0.9 offsets the viscosity ratio", "The residual oil: Sor 0.25 keeps the oil phase connected to the outlet", "The Corey exponents: nw 2.5 pulls the water curve down at every saturation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'M = (krwMax/muW)/(kroMax/muO) = (0.3/0.5)/(0.9/1.8) = 1.2. Water carries only a third of the endpoint kr that oil does, which cancels most of its viscosity advantage. Exponents and connate water shape the interior of the curves but never enter the endpoint definition, and at the endpoints themselves the exponents have no effect at all.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'The flood begins with the sand at its connate water saturation of 0.35. What does the fractional flow equation say about water flow through the rock at that starting saturation?', '["fw = 0.35: the water cut equals the water saturation", "fw = 0.5: both phases start out equally mobile", "fw = 0: krw is zero at Swc, so no water flows", "fw = 1.2: the mobility ratio sets the starting cut"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At connate saturation krw = 0, and the engine returns fw = 0 whenever the water phase cannot move. Water cut is not the same number as water saturation, and the mobility ratio is a curve-set property, not a flow fraction.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'At Sw 0.55 in the Ekene set, what is the normalized water saturation Swn?', '["0.50", "0.20", "0.31", "0.55"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Swn = (Sw - Swc)/(1 - Swc - Sor) = (0.55 - 0.35)/0.40 = 0.50. The 0.20 figure is the numerator left undivided, 0.31 divides by 1 - Swc instead of the mobile span, and 0.55 confuses the raw saturation with its normalized form.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'Using the Ekene Corey set at Sw 0.55, which value is the water relative permeability krw?', '["0.17677669529663687", "0.2249999999999999", "0.075", "0.05303300858899109"]'::jsonb, 3, 'krw = krwMax x Swn^nw = 0.3 x 0.5^2.5 = 0.05303300858899109. The 0.17677669529663687 figure is 0.5^2.5 with the endpoint multiplier forgotten, 0.2249999999999999 is the OIL curve kro at the same saturation, and 0.075 comes from using nw = 2 instead of 2.5.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'At Sw = 0.75, the top of the mobile range in the Ekene sand, what do the two relative permeability curves read?', '["krw 1.0 and kro 0", "krw 0.3 and kro 0", "krw 0.3 and kro 0.9", "krw 0 and kro 0.9"]'::jsonb, 1, '1 - Sor = 0.75 is the flooded-out endpoint: water reaches its endpoint value krwMax = 0.3 while oil is immobile at residual. krw climbs to 1 only on a NORMALIZED plot, and the krw 0 with kro 0.9 pair belongs to the other end of the range, at connate water.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'A lab kr table arrives with krw = 0.05 at its lowest saturation row. What does the engine''s validator do?', '["Accepts it and subtracts 0.05 from every krw value to fix the datum", "Accepts it: a small nonzero krw at connate water is physically required", "Rejects it: krw at the lowest Sw should be 0 because connate water is immobile", "Rejects it only if the kro column also fails its own residual-oil endpoint check at the highest saturation row"]'::jsonb, 2, 'validateKrTable enforces an immobile wetting phase at the lowest saturation and reports exactly that message. The engine never edits the data it is given, and each check fires independently of the others.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Which requirement on a water-oil kr table does the engine actually enforce?', '["kro must be non-increasing as Sw rises", "krw must rise linearly between its endpoints", "kro must reach exactly 0.9 at connate water in every rock", "Rows must be supplied to the engine already sorted by Sw"]'::jsonb, 0, 'More water means less oil mobility, so kro must never rise with Sw, and the validator rejects any table where it does. Curve shape between endpoints is unconstrained, 0.9 is just the Ekene endpoint rather than a law, and the engine sorts incoming rows itself.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Relative permeability is relative to what?', '["The permeability of the other phase at the same saturation", "The rock''s base (absolute) permeability", "The porosity of the core sample measured at the same laboratory conditions", "The viscosity ratio of the two fluids"]'::jsonb, 1, 'kr is the effective permeability of a phase at a given saturation divided by the rock''s single-phase base permeability, which is why every curve lives between 0 and 1. Comparing the two phases to each other gives a mobility ratio, a different object, and viscosity never enters kr at all.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Raising the water Corey exponent nw from 2.5 to 3.0, holding everything else, does what to the krw curve?', '["Raises the endpoint krwMax above 0.3", "Shifts the connate water saturation to higher Sw", "Leaves the curve unchanged away from the endpoints", "Lowers krw at every interior saturation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Swn is a fraction between 0 and 1 in the interior, so a larger exponent shrinks Swn^nw everywhere between the endpoints. The endpoints themselves are pinned by krwMax and by Swc and Sor, none of which the exponent touches.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Which expression gives the Ekene endpoint mobility ratio, and what is its value?', '["1.8 divided by 0.5, giving 3.6", "(0.5/0.3) divided by (1.8/0.9), giving 0.83", "(0.3/0.5) divided by (0.9/1.8), giving 1.2", "(0.3 x 0.5) divided by (0.9 x 1.8), giving 0.093"]'::jsonb, 2, 'M = (krwMax/muW)/(kroMax/muO): the displacing phase''s endpoint mobility over the displaced phase''s. That is (0.6)/(0.5) = 1.2. The bare viscosity ratio 3.6 ignores the rock, and the other forms invert or multiply the terms the wrong way.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'The fractional flow of water at Sw 0.55 in the Ekene sand is which value?', '["0.459029062228061", "0.20920166128387394", "0.8682763300877854", "0.5000000000000001"]'::jsonb, 0, 'fw = 1/(1 + (kro x muW)/(krw x muO)) with krw 0.05303300858899109 and kro 0.2249999999999999 gives 0.459029062228061. The 0.20920166128387394 figure is fw at Sw 0.5, one step down the curve; 0.8682763300877854 is the front value fwf; and 0.5000000000000001 is the normalized saturation, not a flow fraction.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'What pins fw to exactly 0 at one end of the mobile range and exactly 1 at the other?', '["The viscosity ratio of the fluids evaluated at each end of the range", "Mass balance for the water phase integrated across the moving shock front", "The Corey exponents nw and no", "Zero krw at connate water and zero kro at residual oil"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The limits come from the kr endpoints alone: when krw = 0 no water can flow whatever the viscosities, and when kro = 0 the outlet stream is all water. The engine returns the limits directly from those conditions.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'In the textbook sample case (Swc 0.2, Sor 0.2, krwMax 0.4, kroMax 1.0, nw 2, no 2, muW 0.5, muO 5), what is fw at Sw 0.5?', '["0.723261043667905", "0.8", "0.667", "0.5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At Sw 0.5 the closed forms give krw = 0.1 and kro = 0.25, so fw = 1/(1 + (0.25 x 0.5)/(0.1 x 5)) = 1/1.25 = 0.8. The 0.723261043667905 figure is that case''s front value fwf, which lives at a different saturation.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Raising Ekene oil viscosity from 1.8 cp to 10 cp moves the breakthrough displacement efficiency EDbt from 0.5088773453049006 to which value?', '["0.3693282019820123", "0.4299040270894154", "0.49563052575515487", "0.6153846153846154"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At muO 10 the mobility ratio climbs to 6.666666666666667 and the front weakens to Swf 0.5327999999999999, leaving EDbt at 0.3693282019820123. The 0.4299040270894154 figure is the intermediate muO 5 case, 0.49563052575515487 belongs to the nw 2.0 sensitivity, and the ceiling EDmax does not move with viscosity at all.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'Why does the fw curve have its S shape in the Ekene sand?', '["The Welge tangent construction forces an inflection at the front saturation Swf", "Water viscosity falls steadily as its saturation rises, which steepens the middle", "krw is tiny near Swc and kro is tiny near residual, so fw hugs 0 then 1 with a steep middle", "The engine smooths the 102 sampled points with a cubic spline anchored at the two endpoint values of the curve"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The shape is the competition of the two kr curves passed through the fw formula: each end is dominated by one nearly immobile phase. The tangent only reads the curve, the viscosities are constants, and the engine samples the formula pointwise with no smoothing.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'The mobility ratio M = 1.2 for the Ekene flood uses kr values taken where?', '["At the front saturation Swf on both curves", "At Sw 0.55, the midpoint of the mobile range", "Averaged over the whole mobile saturation range between Swc 0.35 and 0.75", "At the endpoints: krwMax behind the flood, kroMax ahead of it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The endpoint convention compares fully swept rock behind the front, where water flows at krwMax, with untouched rock ahead of it, where oil flows at kroMax. No interior or averaged kr value enters the definition.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'The Welge tangent for the Ekene flood is drawn from which point?', '["(0, 0), the origin of the axes", "(0.35, 0): connate water saturation at zero fractional flow", "(0.6372, 0.8682763300877854), the tangency point at the front itself", "(0.75, 1), the residual oil endpoint where the water curve tops out"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The construction anchors at (Swc, 0), the pre-flood state of the rock. The front is where the tangent TOUCHES the curve: it is the output of the construction, never its starting point.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'What is the Ekene front saturation Swf?', '["0.6372", "0.6807702744481854", "0.5964", "0.75"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tangency lands at 0.6372. The 0.6807702744481854 figure is the AVERAGE saturation behind the front at breakthrough, which always exceeds the front value; 0.5964 is the front for the no 3.0 sensitivity case; 0.75 is the top of the mobile range.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Pore volumes injected at breakthrough, QiBt = 0.33077027444818546 for Ekene, comes from which relation?', '["The front saturation minus the connate water saturation, 0.6372 - 0.35 = 0.2872", "fwf divided by the tangent slope", "The reciprocal of the front slope 3.023246274678918", "The movable fraction 0.4 multiplied by the ceiling efficiency EDmax"]'::jsonb, 2, 'QiBt = 1/fw''(Swf), and the tangent slope at the front is 3.023246274678918. Dividing fwf by that slope collapses to Swf - Swc = 0.2872, a saturation difference rather than a pore volume, and the movable-fraction product gives 0.246, which matches nothing in the construction.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'The average water saturation behind the front at breakthrough, SwAvgBt = 0.6807702744481854, is obtained how?', '["The front saturation Swf plus half the height of the saturation shock", "The midpoint of Swc and 1 - Sor", "fwf multiplied by Swf", "Swc + QiBt: 0.35 + 0.33077027444818546"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Welge''s breakthrough relation is SwAvg = Swc + 1/fw''(Swf), and 1/fw''(Swf) is exactly QiBt. The average always sits above the front value because the region behind the front carries saturations from Swf up toward the residual endpoint.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'At the moment of breakthrough the outlet water cut, in reservoir-rate terms, jumps from zero to about which value?', '["0.64", "0.87", "0.46", "1.0"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The front arrives carrying its own fractional flow, fwf = 0.8682763300877854, so the producer goes from dry oil to roughly 87 percent water at once. Reading 0.64 mistakes the front SATURATION for a cut, and the cut only approaches 1.0 much later, as the outlet nears residual oil.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'Why does the engine''s front saturation come out as exactly 0.6372?', '["The tangent search scans 1000 grid steps of 0.0004 and 0.6372 is grid point 718", "The physics of the Ekene sand fixes the front saturation to exactly four decimal places in any consistent unit system", "The engine rounds an exact tangency solution to the nearest 0.0004", "The Corey exponents make the tangency a terminating decimal"]'::jsonb, 0, 'welgeTangent maximizes the secant slope over a discrete scan of the mobile range, and the step is (0.75 - 0.35)/1000 = 0.0004. The reported front is grid-quantized: a setting, not knowledge. Nothing is rounded, because no closed-form solution is ever computed.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'The tangent slope at the Ekene front equals 3.023246274678918. Which other quantity is numerically identical to it by construction?', '["The slope of the krw curve at the front", "The mobility ratio multiplied by fwf", "The secant slope fwf/(Swf - Swc) from the anchor point", "The reciprocal of EDbt"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Tangency means the maximal secant IS the tangent: fw''(Swf) = fwf/(Swf - Swc) = 0.8682763300877854/0.2872. That identity is how the engine finds the front in the first place. The other combinations give 1.04 and 1.97, matching nothing.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'The ultimate displacement efficiency EDmax for the Ekene set is which value, and what alone can change it?', '["0.6153846153846154, moved by the oil viscosity just as strongly as by the endpoint saturations", "0.6153846153846154, moved only by the endpoint saturations Swc and Sor", "0.5088773453049006, moved by anything at all that shifts the position of the Welge front", "0.75, fixed by the Corey exponents"]'::jsonb, 1, 'EDmax = (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc) = 0.4/0.65. In the sensitivity table, muO 10 leaves it untouched while Sor 0.15 lifts it to 0.7692307692307692: only endpoints move the ceiling. The 0.5088773453049006 figure is the breakthrough efficiency, and 0.75 is the textbook case''s ceiling.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Cutting Ekene residual oil from 0.25 to 0.15 changes which pair of numbers?', '["EDmax to 0.7692307692307692 while QiBt stays 0.33077027444818546", "QiBt to 0.4134628430602318 while EDmax stays 0.6153846153846154", "fwf falls to a lower value while EDmax is unchanged", "EDmax to 0.7692307692307692 and QiBt to 0.4134628430602318"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lower residual both raises the ceiling and delays breakthrough: the mobile range widens, so the tangent rides further before touching. In that sensitivity row fwf is unchanged at 0.8682763300877854, which is why neither option blaming fwf survives the table.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'At a steady 8000 barrels of water per day, when does the Ekene flood element break through?', '["2801.3556914263622 days", "1482.5683054081346 days", "926.6051908800841 days", "741.2841527040673 days"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Time is pore volumes times pore volume over rate: 0.33077027444818546 x 22410845.5314109 / 8000 = 926.6051908800841 days. The 2801 figure is a FULL pore volume at that rate, and 1482.57 and 741.28 are breakthrough at 5000 and 10000 bwpd.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Halving the injection rate from 10000 to 5000 bwpd does what to breakthrough?', '["Time doubles to 1482.5683054081346 days; the breakthrough pore volume is unchanged", "Both the breakthrough time and the breakthrough pore volume double together", "The breakthrough time doubles while the breakthrough pore volume is cut in half", "The breakthrough time is unchanged because the pore volume requirement doubles to compensate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'QiBt is a property of the fw curve alone: 0.33077027444818546 pore volumes at any rate. Only the clock changes, inversely with rate, which is exactly what the 741 and 1482 day figures show.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'The engine''s recovery profile ends with Qi = 3837.600651170649 pore volumes at Sw2 = 0.75. What is that number telling you?', '["The fixture''s committed last row is corrupted and should be regenerated", "The flood element needs 3837 days of injection at the design rate to finish", "The engine failed to converge near residual oil", "Reaching residual takes enormous injection: fw'' collapses toward zero at the curve''s end"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each profile row is Qi = 1/fw''(Sw2), and as the curve flattens toward fw = 1 the derivative dies, so the required volumes diverge. It becomes days only after multiplying by pore volume over rate, and nothing failed: the divergence is the physics of the tail.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Displacement efficiency ED measures which of these?', '["Oil recovered from the whole field over its full life as a fraction of the booked STOIIP", "Oil recovered from the swept rock as a fraction of the oil that rock held", "The areal fraction of the well pattern that the injected water has contacted at breakthrough", "The produced water cut at the outlet at a given time"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ED is the one-dimensional pore-scale efficiency inside the swept rock. Areal and vertical sweep are separate factors that belong to pattern-scale flood design, and only the product of all three approaches a field recovery factor.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'After breakthrough, what happens at the producing end as injection continues?', '["The outlet saturation climbs above Swf and the water cut rises along the fw curve toward 1", "The produced water cut holds constant at fwf until residual oil has been reached everywhere in the element", "The front saturation Swf itself increases steadily with continued injection", "ED jumps immediately to its ceiling EDmax"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Post-breakthrough the outlet rides up the fw curve: higher saturations arrive carrying higher cuts. Swf is fixed by the tangent construction once and for all, and ED creeps toward its ceiling only as the slow tail is swept.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Which assumption list matches the engine''s Buckley-Leverett displacement analysis?', '["Three-dimensional, immiscible, capillary pressure included in fw", "One-dimensional, miscible, with the gravity term always switched on", "One-dimensional, immiscible, capillary pressure neglected in fw", "Two-dimensional areal, immiscible, with fully compressible fluids"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The classic construction is a one-dimensional immiscible displacement with capillary pressure neglected in the fractional flow expression. Everything the tangent delivers rests on those simplifications, which is why the course states them rather than hiding them.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'The capstone asks for days to breakthrough at 8000 bwpd. Which two numbers combine with that rate?', '["EDbt 0.5088773453049006 and movable oil 8964338.21256436 bbl", "QiBt 0.33077027444818546 and the pore volume 22410845.5314109 bbl", "SwAvgBt 0.6807702744481854 and the pore volume", "fwf 0.8682763300877854 and QiBt"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The clock is pore volumes times pore volume over rate, nothing else: pvToDays(QiBt, PV, rate). Efficiencies and averages describe what the flood achieves, not when it arrives.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'In the Sor 0.15 sensitivity, Swf moves to 0.709 while fwf stays exactly 0.8682763300877854. What does that reveal?', '["A bug in the engine''s sensitivity table, since two curve sets cannot share a front flow value", "fwf never depends on Sor for any relative permeability curve set", "The tangent construction was accidentally not recomputed for the new Sor", "Corey scaling: the tangent touches the same normalized saturation, so fw repeats"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Changing Sor re-maps the curve along the saturation axis but the kr RATIO at a given normalized saturation is unchanged, so the tangent touches at the same Swn and carries the same fw. It is a property of this parameterization, not a general law, so the blanket claim fails.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Comparing the textbook case (M = 4) with Ekene (M = 1.2), which pairing of breakthrough efficiency is right?', '["Textbook 0.46352558724832216, Ekene 0.5088773453049006: better mobility displaces more", "Textbook 0.75 and Ekene 0.6153846153846154: the ceilings are the breakthrough efficiencies", "Textbook 0.5088773453049006 and Ekene 0.46352558724832216: the heavier oil case wins", "Both 0.5: breakthrough efficiency is the same for every curve set"]'::jsonb, 0, 'EDbt is case-specific and favorable mobility strengthens the front, so Ekene''s 1.2 beats the textbook''s 4. The 0.75 and 0.6153846153846154 figures are the EDmax ceilings, which are endpoint properties, not breakthrough results.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Which statement about the engine''s Welge numbers is a SETTING rather than knowledge about the rock?', '["Water cannot flow anywhere in the element at connate water saturation", "The average saturation behind the front exceeds the front value", "The front saturation is reported on a 0.0004 saturation grid", "Displacement efficiency can never beat the ceiling that the endpoint saturations set"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The 0.0004 step is just the n = 1000 scan resolution, a configurable choice. The other three statements are physics of the construction and would survive any grid.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The Ekene displacement numbers in this course reconcile with the geoscience booking how?', '["The course re-derives STOIIP from the kr curves as an independent check", "The two are independent bookkeeping systems and need not agree", "The booking''s Bo of 1.2 sets the mobility ratio of the flood", "The flood works on the same pore volume NG5 booked, with Swi equal to the kr set''s connate 0.35"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Volumetrics owns HOW MUCH oil is there; SCAL adds HOW it moves. The shared pore volume 22410845.5314109 bbl and the shared 0.35 saturation are what tie the two courses into one field story, and neither course re-derives the other''s numbers.'),
  ('scal', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'A colleague reports that the Ekene flood recovers 61.5 percent of the displaceable-zone oil at breakthrough. What went wrong?', '["Nothing: breakthrough recovery is 61.5 percent", "They quoted the ceiling EDmax 0.6153846153846154 instead of EDbt 0.5088773453049006", "They read the front saturation 0.6372 off the tangent chart and reported it as if it were a recovery", "They mixed in the textbook case''s numbers by mistake"]'::jsonb, 1, 'EDmax is the never-quite-reached ceiling set by the endpoints; at breakthrough the efficiency is 0.5088773453049006. The front saturation would have read 63.7 percent, not 61.5, and the textbook case''s numbers are 0.46352558724832216 and 0.75, so neither alternative explains the quote.');
